sn22.87:2.1 | S: Then the Buddha robed up and, taking his bowl and robes, went to Venerable Vakkali. | T: Then the Blessed One dressed and, taking bowl and robe, approached the Venerable Vakkali.
sn22.87:2.2 | S: Venerable Vakkali saw the Buddha coming off in the distance and stirred in his cot. | T: The Venerable Vakkali saw the Blessed One coming in the distance and stirred on his bed.
sn22.87:2.3 | S: But the Buddha said to him, | T: The Blessed One said to him:
sn22.87:2.4 | S: “It’s all right, Vakkali, don’t get up. | T: “Enough, Vakkali, do not stir on your bed.
sn22.87:2.5 | S: There are some seats spread out, I will sit there.” | T: There are these seats ready, I will sit down there.”
sn22.87:2.6 | S: He sat down on the seat spread out and said to Vakkali, | T: The Blessed One then sat down on the appointed seat and said to the Venerable Vakkali:
sn22.87:2.7 | S: “I hope you’re keeping well, Vakkali; I hope you’re all right. I hope your pain is fading, not growing; that its fading is evident, not its growing.” | T: “I hope you are bearing up, Vakkali, I hope you are getting better. I hope that your painful feelings are subsiding and not increasing, and that their subsiding, not their increase, is to be discerned.”
sn22.87:2.8 | S: “Sir, I’m not keeping well, I’m not getting by. My pain is terrible and growing, not fading; its growing is evident, not its fading.” | T: “Venerable sir, I am not bearing up, I am not getting better. Strong painful feelings are increasing in me, not subsiding, and their increase, not their subsiding, is to be discerned.”
sn22.87:2.9 | S: “I hope you don’t have any remorse or regret?” | T: “I hope then, Vakkali, that you are not troubled by remorse and regret.”
sn22.87:2.10 | S: “Indeed, sir, I have no little remorse and regret.” | T: “Indeed, venerable sir, I have quite a lot of remorse and regret.”
sn22.87:2.11 | S: “I hope you have no reason to blame yourself when it comes to ethical conduct?” | T: “I hope, Vakkali, that you have nothing for which to reproach yourself in regard to virtue.”
