sn22.87:8.10 | P: Bhagavā ca taṁ, āvuso vakkali, evamāha: | T: And the Blessed One says to you, friend Vakkali:
sn22.87:8.11 | P: ‘mā bhāyi, vakkali; | T: ‘Do not be afraid, Vakkali,
sn22.87:8.12 | P: mā bhāyi, vakkali. | T: do not be afraid!
sn22.87:8.13 | P: Apāpakaṁ te maraṇaṁ bhavissati, apāpikā kālakiriyā’”ti. | T: Your death will not be a bad one. Your demise will not be a bad one.’”
sn22.87:8.14 | S: Vakkali said, “Well then, reverends, in my name bow with your head to the Buddha’s feet. Say to him: | T: “Well then, friends, pay homage to the Blessed One in my name with your head at his feet and say:
sn22.87:8.15 | S: ‘Sir, the mendicant Vakkali is sick, suffering, gravely ill. | T: ‘Venerable sir, the bhikkhu Vakkali is sick, afflicted, gravely ill;
sn22.87:8.16 | S: He bows with his head to your feet.’ | T: he pays homage to the Blessed One with his head at his feet.’
sn22.87:8.17 | S: And then say: | T: Then say:
sn22.87:8.18 | S: ‘Form is impermanent. | T: ‘Form is impermanent:
sn22.87:8.19 | S: I have no doubt of that. | T: I have no perplexity about this, venerable sir,
sn22.87:8.20 | S: I’m certain that what is impermanent is suffering. | T: I do not doubt that whatever is impermanent is suffering.
sn22.87:8.21 | S: And I’m certain that I have no desire or greed or fondness for what is impermanent, suffering, and perishable. | T: I do not doubt that in regard to what is impermanent, suffering, and subject to change, I have no more desire, lust, or affection.
sn22.87:8.22 | S: Feeling is impermanent … | T: Feeling is impermanent …
sn22.87:8.23 | P: Tāhaṁ, bhante, na kaṅkhāmi. | T: (none)
sn22.87:8.24 | P: Yadaniccaṁ taṁ dukkhanti na vicikicchāmi. | T: (none)
sn22.87:8.25 | P: Yadaniccaṁ dukkhaṁ vipariṇāmadhammaṁ, natthi me tattha chando vā rāgo vā pemaṁ vāti na vicikicchāmi. | T: (none)
sn22.87:8.26 | S: Perception is impermanent … | T: Perception is impermanent …
sn22.87:8.27 | S: Choices are impermanent … | T: Volitional formations are impermanent …
sn22.87:8.28 | P: Tāhaṁ, bhante, na kaṅkhāmi. | T: (none)
sn22.87:8.29 | P: Yadaniccaṁ taṁ dukkhanti na vicikicchāmi. | T: (none)
sn22.87:8.30 | P: Yadaniccaṁ dukkhaṁ vipariṇāmadhammaṁ, natthi me tattha chando vā rāgo vā pemaṁ vāti na vicikicchāmi. | T: (none)
sn22.87:8.31 | S: Consciousness is impermanent. | T: Consciousness is impermanent:
sn22.87:8.32 | S: I have no doubt of that. | T: I have no perplexity about this, venerable sir,
sn22.87:8.33 | S: I’m certain that what is impermanent is suffering. | T: I do not doubt that whatever is impermanent is suffering.
sn22.87:8.34 | S: And I’m certain that I have no desire or greed or fondness for what is impermanent, suffering, and perishable.’” | T: I do not doubt that in regard to what is impermanent, suffering, and subject to change, I have no more desire, lust, or affection.’”
sn22.87:8.35 | S: “Yes, reverend,” those monks replied, and left. | T: “Yes, friend,” those bhikkhus replied, and then they departed.
sn22.87:8.36 | S: And then, not long after those mendicants had left, Venerable Vakkali committed suicide. | T: Then, not long after those bhikkhus had left, the Venerable Vakkali used the knife.
sn22.87:9.1 | S: Then those mendicants went up to the Buddha and told him Vakkali’s message. | T: Then those bhikkhus approached the Blessed One … and delivered their message.
sn22.87:9.2 | P: “vakkali, bhante, bhikkhu ābādhiko dukkhito bāḷhagilāno; | T: (none)
sn22.87:9.3 | P: so bhagavato pāde sirasā vandati; | T: (none)
sn22.87:9.4 | P: evañca vadeti: | T: (none)
sn22.87:9.5 | P: ‘rūpaṁ aniccaṁ. | T: (none)
sn22.87:9.6 | P: Tāhaṁ, bhante, na kaṅkhāmi. | T: (none)
sn22.87:9.7 | P: Yadaniccaṁ taṁ dukkhanti na vicikicchāmi. | T: (none)
sn22.87:9.8 | P: Yadaniccaṁ dukkhaṁ vipariṇāmadhammaṁ, natthi me tattha chando vā rāgo vā pemaṁ vāti na vicikicchāmi. | T: (none)
sn22.87:9.9 | P: Vedanā … | T: (none)
sn22.87:9.10 | P: saññā … | T: (none)
sn22.87:9.11 | P: saṅkhārā … | T: (none)
sn22.87:9.12 | P: viññāṇaṁ aniccaṁ. | T: (none)
sn22.87:9.13 | P: Tāhaṁ, bhante, na kaṅkhāmi. | T: (none)
sn22.87:9.14 | P: Yadaniccaṁ taṁ dukkhanti na vicikicchāmi. | T: (none)
sn22.87:9.15 | P: Yadaniccaṁ dukkhaṁ vipariṇāmadhammaṁ, natthi me tattha chando vā rāgo vā pemaṁ vāti na vicikicchāmī’”ti. | T: (none)
sn22.87:10.1 | S: Then the Buddha said to the mendicants, | T: The Blessed One then addressed the bhikkhus thus:
sn22.87:10.2 | S: “Come, mendicants, let’s go to the Black Rock on the slopes of Isigili, | T: “Come, bhikkhus, let us go to the Black Rock on the Isigili Slope,
sn22.87:10.3 | S: where Vakkali, the gentleman, committed suicide.” | T: where the clansman Vakkali has used the knife.”
sn22.87:10.4 | S: “Yes, sir,” they replied. | T: “Yes, venerable sir,” those bhikkhus replied.
sn22.87:10.5 | S: Then the Buddha together with several mendicants went to the Black Rock on the slopes of Isigili. | T: Then the Blessed One, together with a number of bhikkhus, went to the Black Rock on the Isigili Slope.
sn22.87:10.6 | S: The Buddha saw Vakkali off in the distance on his cot, lying with aggregates cast off. | T: The Blessed One saw in the distance the Venerable Vakkali lying on the bed with his shoulder turned.
sn22.87:11.1 | S: Now at that time a cloud of black smoke was moving east, west, north, south, above, below, and in-between. | T: Now on that occasion a cloud of smoke, a swirl of darkness, was moving to the east, then to the west, to the north, to the south, upwards, downwards, and to the intermediate quarters.
sn22.87:11.2 | S: The Buddha said to the mendicants, | T: The Blessed One then addressed the bhikkhus thus:
sn22.87:11.3 | S: “Mendicants, do you see that cloud of black smoke moving east, west, north, south, above, below, and in-between?” | T: “Do you see, bhikkhus, that cloud of smoke, that swirl of darkness, moving to the east, then to the west, to the north, to the south, upwards, downwards, and to the intermediate quarters?”
sn22.87:11.4 | S: “Yes, sir.” | T: “Yes, venerable sir.”
sn22.87:11.5 | S: “That’s Māra the Wicked searching for Vakkali’s consciousness, wondering: | T: “That, bhikkhus, is Mara the Evil One searching for the consciousness of the clansman Vakkali, wondering:
sn22.87:11.6 | S: ‘Where is Vakkali’s consciousness established?’ | T: ‘Where now has the consciousness of the clansman Vakkali been established?’
sn22.87:11.7 | S: But since his consciousness is not established, Vakkali is quenched.” | T: However, bhikkhus, with consciousness unestablished, the clansman Vakkali has attained final Nibbāna.”
sn22.87:11.8 | P: Pañcamaṁ. | T: (none)

