## sn11.1
sn11.1:1.1 | S: So I have heard. | T: Thus have I heard.
sn11.1:1.2 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying near Sāvatthī in Jeta’s Grove, Anāthapiṇḍika’s monastery. | T: On one occasion the Blessed One was dwelling at Savatthī in Jeta’s Grove, Anathapiṇḍika’s Park.
sn11.1:1.3 | S: There the Buddha addressed the mendicants, | T: There the Blessed One addressed the bhikkhus thus:
sn11.1:1.4 | S: “Mendicants!” | T: “Bhikkhus!”
sn11.1:1.5 | S: “Venerable sir,” they replied. | T: “Venerable sir!” those bhikkhus replied.
sn11.1:1.6 | S: The Buddha said this: | T: The Blessed One said this:
sn11.1:2.1 | S: “Once upon a time, mendicants, the titans marched against the gods. | T: “Bhikkhus, once in the past the asuras marched against the devas.
sn11.1:2.2 | S: Then Sakka, lord of gods, addressed the godling Suvīra, | T: Then Sakka, lord of the devas, addressed Suvīra, a young deva, thus:
sn11.1:2.3 | S: ‘Dear Suvīra, the titans march against the gods! | T: ‘Dear Suvīra, these asuras are marching against the devas.
sn11.1:2.4 | S: Go, and march against the titans!’ | T: Go, dear Suvīra, launch a counter-march against the asuras.’—
sn11.1:2.5 | S: ‘Yes, lord,’ replied Suvīra. But he fell into negligence. | T: ‘Yes, your lordship,’ Suvīra replied, but he became negligent.
sn11.1:2.6 | S: For a second time Sakka addressed Suvīra, | T: A second time Sakka addressed Suvīra …
sn11.1:2.7 | S: ‘Dear Suvīra, the titans march against the gods! | T: (none)
sn11.1:2.8 | S: Go, and march against the titans!’ | T: …
sn11.1:2.9 | S: ‘Yes, lord,’ replied Suvīra. But for a second time he fell into negligence. | T: but a second time Suvīra became negligent.
sn11.1:2.10 | S: For a third time Sakka addressed Suvīra, | T: A third time Sakka addressed Suvīra …
sn11.1:2.11 | S: ‘Dear Suvīra, the titans march against the gods! | T: (none)
sn11.1:2.12 | S: Go, and march against the titans!’ | T: (none)
sn11.1:2.13 | S: ‘Yes, lord,’ replied Suvīra. But for a third time he fell into negligence. | T: but a third time Suvīra became negligent.
sn11.1:2.14 | S: Then Sakka addressed the godling Suvīra in verse: | T: Then, bhikkhus, Sakka addressed Suvīra in verse:
sn11.1:3.1 | S: ‘Suvīra, go to that place | T: “‘Where one need not toil and strive
sn11.1:3.2 | S: where you can achieve happiness | T: Yet still may attain to bliss:
sn11.1:3.3 | S: without working for it or trying hard— | T: Go there, Suvīra,
sn11.1:3.4 | S: and take me with you!’ | T: And take me along with you.’
sn11.1:4.1 | S: ‘That a lazy man who does no work, | T: Suvīra: “‘That a lazy man who does not toil
sn11.1:4.2 | S: and doesn’t do his duty, | T: Nor attend to his duties
sn11.1:4.3 | S: can fulfill all his desires: | T: Might still have all desires fulfilled:
sn11.1:4.4 | S: Sakka, render me this boon!’ | T: Grant me that, Sakka, as a boon.’
sn11.1:5.1 | S: ‘Suvīra, go to that place | T: Sakka: “‘Where a lazy man who does not toil
sn11.1:5.2 | S: where a lazy man who does no work | T: (none)
sn11.1:5.3 | S: prospers in unending happiness— | T: Might achieve unending bliss: Go there, Suvīra,
sn11.1:5.4 | S: and take me with you!’ | T: And take me along with you.’
sn11.1:6.1 | S: ‘O Sakka, first among gods, | T: Suvīra: “‘The bliss, supreme deva,
sn11.1:6.2 | S: that we might find the happiness | T: we might find Without doing work, O Sakka,
sn11.1:6.3 | S: that’s sorrowless, unstressed: | T: The sorrowless state without despair:
sn11.1:6.4 | S: Sakka, render me this boon!’ | T: Grant me that, Sakka, as a boon.’
sn11.1:7.1 | S: ‘If there exists anywhere a place | T: Sakka: “‘If there exists any place anywhere
sn11.1:7.2 | S: where one can live happily without working, | T: Where without work one won’t decline,
sn11.1:7.3 | S: that surely would be extinguishment’s path! | T: That is indeed Nibbāna’s path:
sn11.1:7.4 | S: Go there, Suvīra, | T: Go there, Suvīra,
sn11.1:7.5 | S: and take me with you!’ | T: And take me along with you.’
sn11.1:8.1 | S: Since, mendicants, even that Sakka, lord of gods—while living off of the fruit of his good deeds, and ruling as sovereign lord over these gods of the thirty-three—will speak in praise of initiative and energy, | T: “So, bhikkhus, if Sakka, lord of the devas, subsisting on the fruit of his own merit, exercising supreme sovereignty and rulership over the Tavatiṁsa devas, will be one who speaks in praise of initiative and energy,
sn11.1:8.2 | S: you can excel at this, for you who have gone forth in such a well explained teaching and training can try hard, strive, and make an effort to attain the unattained, achieve the unachieved, and realize the unrealized.” | T: then how much more would it be fitting here for you, who have gone forth in such a well-expounded Dhamma and Discipline, to toil, struggle, and strive for the attainment of the as-yet-unattained, for the achievement of the as-yet-unachieved, for the realization of the as-yet-unrealized.”

## an3.57
an3.57:1.1 | S: Then the wanderer Vacchagotta went up to the Buddha, and exchanged greetings with him. | T: Then the wanderer Vacchagotta approached the Blessed One … and said to him:
an3.57:1.2 | S: When the greetings and polite conversation were over, he sat down to one side and said to the Buddha: | T: (none)
an3.57:1.3 | S: “I have heard, worthy Gotama, that the ascetic Gotama says this: | T: “Master Gotama, I have heard: ‘The ascetic Gotama says:
an3.57:1.4 | S: ‘Gifts should only be given to me, not to others. | T: “Alms should be given only to me, not to others;
an3.57:1.5 | S: Gifts should only be given to my disciples, not to the disciples of others. | T: alms should be given only to my disciples, not to the disciples of others.
an3.57:1.6 | S: Only what is given to me is very fruitful, not what is given to others. | T: Only what is given to me is very fruitful, not what is given to others;
an3.57:1.7 | S: Only what is given to my disciples is very fruitful, not what is given to the disciples of others.’ | T: only what is given to my disciples is very fruitful, not what is given to the disciples of others.”’
an3.57:1.8 | P: Ye te, bho gotama, evamāhaṁsu ‘samaṇo gotamo evamāha: | T: (none)
an3.57:1.9 | P: “mayhameva dānaṁ dātabbaṁ, nāññesaṁ dānaṁ dātabbaṁ. | T: (none)
an3.57:1.10 | P: Mayhameva sāvakānaṁ dānaṁ dātabbaṁ, nāññesaṁ sāvakānaṁ dānaṁ dātabbaṁ. | T: (none)
an3.57:1.11 | P: Mayhameva dinnaṁ mahapphalaṁ, nāññesaṁ dinnaṁ mahapphalaṁ. | T: (none)
an3.57:1.12 | P: Mayhameva sāvakānaṁ dinnaṁ mahapphalaṁ, nāññesaṁ sāvakānaṁ dinnaṁ mahapphalan”’ti. | T: (none)
an3.57:1.13 | S: I trust those who say this repeat what the worthy Gotama has said, and do not misrepresent him with an untruth? Is their explanation in line with the teaching? Are there any legitimate grounds for rebuttal and criticism? | T: Do those who speak thus state what has been said by Master Gotama and not misrepresent him with what is contrary to fact? Do they explain in accordance with the Dhamma so that they would not incur any reasonable criticism or ground for censure?
an3.57:1.14 | S: For we don’t want to misrepresent the worthy Gotama.” | T: For we do not want to misrepresent Master Gotama.”
an3.57:2.1 | S: “Vaccha, those who say this | T: “Those, Vaccha, who say:
an3.57:2.2 | P: ‘samaṇo gotamo evamāha: | T: ‘The ascetic Gotama says:
an3.57:2.3 | P: “mayhameva dānaṁ dātabbaṁ …pe… | T: “Alms should be given only to me …
an3.57:2.4 | P: nāññesaṁ sāvakānaṁ dinnaṁ mahapphalan”’ti | T: only what is given to my disciples is very fruitful, not what is given to the disciples of others,”’
an3.57:2.5 | S: do not repeat what I have said. They misrepresent me with what is false and untrue. | T: do not state what has been said by me but misrepresent me with what is untrue and contrary to fact.
an3.57:2.6 | S: Anyone who prevents another from giving makes an obstacle and a roadblock for three individuals. | T: One who prevents another from giving alms creates an obstruction and stumbling block for three people.
an3.57:2.7 | S: What three? | T: What three?
an3.57:2.8 | S: The giver is obstructed from making merit. The receiver is obstructed from getting what is offered. And they’ve already broken and damaged themselves. | T: He creates an obstruction to the donor’s acquiring merit, to the recipients’ gaining a gift, and already he has maimed and injured himself.
an3.57:2.9 | S: Anyone who prevents another from giving makes an obstacle and a roadblock for these three individuals. | T: One who prevents another from giving alms creates an obstruction and stumbling block for these three people.
an3.57:3.1 | S: Vaccha, this is what I say: | T: “But, Vaccha, I say that one acquires merit even if one throws away dishwashing water in a refuse dump or cesspit with the thought:
an3.57:3.2 | S: ‘You even make merit by tipping out dish-washing water in a cesspool or a sump with living creatures in it, thinking, | T: (none)
an3.57:3.3 | S: “May any creatures here be nourished!”’ | T: ‘May the living beings here sustain themselves with this!’
an3.57:3.4 | S: How much more then for human beings! | T: How much more, then, does one acquire merit when one gives to human beings!
an3.57:3.5 | S: However, I also say that a gift to an ethical person is more fruitful than one to an unethical person. They’ve given up five factors, and possess five factors. | T: However, I say that what is given to one of virtuous behavior is more fruitful than what is given to an immoral person. And the most worthy recipient is one who has abandoned five factors and possesses five factors.
an3.57:4.1 | S: What are the five factors they’ve given up? | T: “What five factors has he abandoned?
an3.57:4.2 | S: Sensual desire, ill will, dullness and drowsiness, restlessness and remorse, and doubt. | T: Sensual desire, ill will, dullness and drowsiness, restlessness and remorse, and doubt.
an3.57:4.3 | S: These are the five factors they’ve given up. | T: These are the five factors that he has abandoned.
an3.57:5.1 | S: What are the five factors they possess? | T: “And what five factors does he possess?
an3.57:5.2 | S: The entire spectrum of an adept’s ethics, immersion, wisdom, freedom, and knowledge and vision of freedom. | T: The virtuous behavior, concentration, wisdom, liberation, and knowledge and vision of liberation of one beyond training.
an3.57:5.3 | S: These are the five factors they possess. | T: These are the five factors that he possesses.
an3.57:5.4 | S: I say that a gift to anyone who has given up these five factors and possesses these five factors is very fruitful. | T: “It is in such a way, I say, that what is given to one who has abandoned five factors and possesses five factors is very fruitful.”
an3.57:6.1 | S: Cows may be black or white, | T: Among cattle of any sort, whether black, white,
an3.57:6.2 | S: red or tawny, | T: red, or golden,
an3.57:6.3 | S: mottled or uniform, | T: mottled, uniform,
an3.57:6.4 | S: or pigeon-colored. | T: or pigeon-colored,
an3.57:7.1 | S: But when one is born among them, | T: the tamed bull is born,
an3.57:7.2 | S: the bull that’s tamed— | T: (none)
an3.57:7.3 | S: a behemoth, powerful, | T: the one that can bear the load, possessing strength,
an3.57:7.4 | S: well-paced in pulling forward— | T: advancing with good speed.
an3.57:7.5 | S: they yoke the load just to him, | T: They yoke the burden just to him;
an3.57:7.6 | S: regardless of his color. | T: they are not concerned about his color.
an3.57:8.1 | S: So it is for humans, | T: So too, among human beings
an3.57:8.2 | S: wherever they may be born— | T: it is in any kind of birth—
an3.57:8.3 | S: among aristocrats, brahmins, peasants, | T: among khattiyas, brahmins, vessas,
an3.57:8.4 | S: menials, or corpse-workers and scavengers— | T: suddas, caṇḍālas, or scavengers—
an3.57:9.1 | S: one is born among them, | T: among people of any sort
an3.57:9.2 | S: tamed, true to their vows. | T: that the tamed person of good manners is born:
an3.57:9.3 | S: Firm in principle, accomplished in ethical conduct, | T: one firm in Dhamma, virtuous in conduct,
an3.57:9.4 | S: truthful, conscientious, | T: truthful in speech, endowed with moral shame;
an3.57:10.1 | S: they’ve given up birth and death. | T: one who has abandoned birth and death,
an3.57:10.2 | S: Complete in the spiritual journey, | T: consummate in the spiritual life,
an3.57:10.3 | S: with burden put down, detached, | T: with the burden dropped, detached,
an3.57:10.4 | S: they’ve completed the task <j>and are free of defilements. | T: who has done his task, free of taints;
an3.57:11.1 | S: Gone beyond all things, | T: who has gone beyond all things of the world
an3.57:11.2 | S: they’re quenched by not grasping. | T: and by non-clinging has reached nibbāna:
an3.57:11.3 | S: In that flawless field, | T: an offering is truly vast when planted in that spotless field.
an3.57:11.4 | S: a religious donation is abundant. | T: (none)
an3.57:12.1 | S: Fools who don’t understand— | T: Fools devoid of understanding,
an3.57:12.2 | S: unlearned simpletons— | T: dull-witted, unlearned,
an3.57:12.3 | S: give their gifts to those outside, | T: do not attend on the holy ones but give their gifts to those outside.
an3.57:12.4 | S: and don’t attend the peaceful ones. | T: (none)
an3.57:13.1 | S: But those who do attend the peaceful ones— | T: Those, however, who attend on the holy ones, on the wise ones esteemed as sagely,
an3.57:13.2 | S: wise, esteemed as attentive— | T: (none)
an3.57:13.3 | S: and whose faith in the Holy One | T: and those whose faith in the Fortunate One
an3.57:13.4 | S: has roots planted deep, | T: is deeply rooted and well established,
an3.57:14.1 | S: they go to the realm of the gods, | T: go to the world of the devas
an3.57:14.2 | S: or are born here in a good family. | T: or are born here in a good family.
an3.57:14.3 | S: Gradually those astute ones | T: Advancing in successive steps,
an3.57:14.4 | S: reach extinguishment.” | T: those wise ones attain nibbāna.
an3.57:14.5 | P: Sattamaṁ. | T: (none)

## an4.55
an4.55:1.1 | S: So I have heard. | T: (none)
an4.55:1.2 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying in the land of the Bhaggas at Crocodile’s Bellow, in the deer park at Bhesakaḷā’s Wood. | T: On one occasion the Blessed One was dwelling among the Bhaggas in Suṁsumāragira in the deer park at Bhesakalā Grove.
an4.55:1.3 | S: Then the Buddha robed up in the morning and, taking his bowl and robe, went to the home of the householder Nakula’s father, where he sat down on the seat spread out. | T: Then, in the morning, the Blessed One dressed, took his bowl and robe, and went to the residence of the householder Nakulapitā, where he sat down in the prepared seat.
an4.55:1.4 | S: Then the householder Nakula’s father and the housewife Nakula’s mother went up to the Buddha, bowed, and sat down to one side. Nakula’s father said to the Buddha, | T: Then the householder Nakulapitā and the housewife Nakulamātā approached the Blessed One, paid homage to him, and sat down to one side. The householder Nakulapitā then said to the Blessed One:
an4.55:2.1 | S: “Sir, ever since we were both young, and Nakula’s mother was given to me in marriage, I can’t recall betraying her even in thought, still less in deed. | T: “Bhante, since I was young, when the young girl Nakulamātā was given to me in marriage, I do not recall ever transgressing against her even in thought, much less by deed.
an4.55:2.2 | S: We want to see each other in both this life and the next.” | T: We wish, Bhante, to see one another not only in this present life but also in future lives.”
an4.55:2.3 | S: Then Nakula’s mother said to the Buddha, | T: The housewife Nakulamātā in turn said to the Blessed One:
an4.55:2.4 | S: “Sir, ever since we were both young, and I was given in marriage to Nakula’s father, I can’t recall betraying him even in thought, still less in deed. | T: “Bhante, since I was a young girl given to the young householder Nakulapitā in marriage, I do not recall ever transgressing against him even in thought, much less by deed.
an4.55:2.5 | S: We want to see each other in both this life and the next.” | T: We wish, Bhante, to see one another not only in this present life but also in future lives.”
an4.55:3.1 | S: “Householders, if wife and husband want to see each other in both this life and the next, they should be equals in faith, ethics, generosity, and wisdom. | T: “Householders, if both husband and wife wish to see one another not only in this present life but also in future lives, they should have the same faith, the same virtuous behavior, the same generosity, and the same wisdom. Then they will see one another not only in this present life but also in future lives.”
an4.55:4.1 | S: When both are faithful and bountiful, | T: Both husband and wife are endowed with faith,
an4.55:4.2 | S: disciplined, living righteously, | T: charitable and self-controlled, living their lives righteously,
an4.55:4.3 | S: then wife and husband | T: (none)
an4.55:4.4 | S: say nice things to each other. | T: addressing each other with pleasant words,
an4.55:5.1 | S: Their needs are amply satisfied, | T: Then many benefits accrue to them
an4.55:5.2 | S: so they live at ease. | T: and they dwell at ease.
an4.55:5.3 | S: Their enemies are downhearted, | T: Their enemies are saddened
an4.55:5.4 | S: when both are equal in ethics. | T: when both are the same in virtue.
an4.55:6.1 | S: Having practiced the teaching here, | T: Having practiced the Dhamma here,
an4.55:6.2 | S: both equal in precepts and observances, | T: the same in virtuous behavior and observances,
an4.55:6.3 | S: they delight in the heavenly realm, | T: delighting after death in a deva world,
an4.55:6.4 | S: enjoying all the pleasures they desire.” | T: they rejoice, enjoying sensual pleasures.
an4.55:6.5 | P: Pañcamaṁ. | T: (none)

## an8.31
an8.31:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, there are these eight gifts. | T: “Bhikkhus, there are these eight gifts.
an8.31:1.2 | S: What eight? | T: What eight?
an8.31:1.3 | S: Someone might give a gift after insulting the recipient. | T: (1) Having insulted the recipient, one gives a gift.
an8.31:1.4 | S: Or they give out of fear. | T: (2) One gives a gift from fear.
an8.31:1.5 | S: Or they give thinking, ‘They gave to me.’ | T: (3) One gives a gift, thinking: ‘He gave to me.’
an8.31:1.6 | S: Or they give thinking, ‘They’ll give to me.’ | T: (4) One gives a gift, thinking: ‘He will give to me.’
an8.31:1.7 | S: Or they give thinking, ‘It’s good to give.’ | T: (5) One gives a gift, thinking: ‘Giving is good.’
an8.31:1.8 | S: Or they give thinking, ‘I cook, they don’t. It wouldn’t be right for me to not give to them.’ | T: (6) One gives a gift, thinking: ‘I cook; these people do not cook. It isn’t right that I who cook should not give to those who do not cook.’
an8.31:1.9 | S: Or they give thinking, ‘By giving this gift I’ll get a good reputation.’ | T: (7) One gives a gift, thinking: ‘Because I have given this gift, I will gain a good reputation.’
an8.31:1.10 | S: Or they give thinking, ‘This is an adornment and requisite for the mind.’ | T: (8) One gives a gift for the purpose of ornamenting the mind, equipping the mind.”
an8.31:1.11 | S: These are the eight gifts.” | T: (none)
an8.31:1.12 | P: Paṭhamaṁ. | T: (none)

## sn35.130
sn35.130:1.1 | S: At one time Venerable Mahākaccāna was staying in the land of the Avantis near Kuraraghara on Steep Mountain. | T: Thus have I heard. On one occasion the Venerable Mahakaccana was dwelling among the people of Avanti on Mount Papata at Kuraraghara.
sn35.130:1.2 | S: Then the householder Hāliddikāni went up to Venerable Mahākaccāna … and asked him, | T: Then the householder Haliddakani approached the Venerable Mahakaccana … and said to him:
sn35.130:1.3 | S: “Sir, this was said by the Buddha: | T: “Venerable sir, it was said by the Blessed One:
sn35.130:1.4 | S: ‘Diversity of elements gives rise to diversity of contacts, and diversity of contacts gives rise to diversity of feelings.’ | T: ‘It is in dependence on the diversity of elements that there arises the diversity of contacts; in dependence on the diversity of contacts that there arises the diversity of feelings.’
sn35.130:1.5 | S: How does diversity of elements give rise to diversity of contacts, and diversity of contacts gives rise to diversity of feelings?” | T: How is this so, venerable sir?”
sn35.130:1.6 | S: “Householder, it’s when a mendicant sees a sight with their eyes and understands it to be agreeable. | T: “Here, householder, having seen a form with the eye, a bhikkhu understands an agreeable one thus: ‘Such it is!’
sn35.130:1.7 | S: There is eye consciousness; and pleasant feeling arises dependent on a contact to be experienced as pleasant. | T: There is eye-consciousness, and in dependence on a contact to be experienced as pleasant there arises a pleasant feeling.
sn35.130:1.8 | S: Then they see a sight and understand it to be disagreeable. | T: Then, having seen a form with the eye, a bhikkhu understands a disagreeable one thus: ‘Such it is!’
sn35.130:1.9 | S: There is eye consciousness; and painful feeling arises dependent on a contact to be experienced as painful. | T: There is eye-consciousness, and in dependence on a contact to be experienced as painful there arises a painful feeling.
sn35.130:1.10 | S: Then they see a sight and understand it to be a basis for equanimity. | T: Then, having seen a form with the eye, a bhikkhu understands one that is a basis for equanimity thus: ‘Such it is!’
sn35.130:1.11 | S: There is eye consciousness; and neutral feeling arises dependent on a contact to be experienced as neutral. | T: There is eye-consciousness, and in dependence on a contact to be experienced as neither-painful-nor-pleasant there arises a neither-painful-nor-pleasant feeling.
sn35.130:2.1 | S: Furthermore, a mendicant hears a sound with their ears … | T: “Further, householder, having heard a sound with the ear …
sn35.130:2.2 | S: smells an odor with their nose … | T: having smelt an odour with the nose …
sn35.130:2.3 | S: tastes a flavor with their tongue … | T: having savoured a taste with the tongue …
sn35.130:2.4 | S: encounters a touch with their body … | T: having felt a tactile object with the body …
sn35.130:2.5 | S: knows an idea with their mind and understands it to be agreeable. | T: having cognized a mental phenomenon with the mind, a bhikkhu understands an agreeable one thus … …
sn35.130:2.6 | S: There is mind consciousness; and pleasant feeling arises dependent on a contact to be experienced as pleasant. | T: (none)
sn35.130:2.7 | S: Then they know an idea and understand it to be disagreeable. | T: a disagreeable one thus …
sn35.130:2.8 | S: There is mind consciousness; and painful feeling arises dependent on a contact to be experienced as painful. | T: (none)
sn35.130:2.9 | S: Then they know an idea and understand it to be a basis for equanimity. | T: one that is a basis for equanimity thus: ‘Such it is!’
sn35.130:2.10 | S: Neutral feeling arises dependent on a contact to be experienced as neutral. | T: There is mind-consciousness, and in dependence on a contact to be experienced as neither-painful-nor-pleasant there arises a neither-painful-nor-pleasant feeling.
sn35.130:2.11 | S: That’s how diversity of elements gives rise to diversity of contacts, and diversity of contacts gives rise to diversity of feelings.” | T: “It is in this way, householder, that in dependence on the diversity of elements there arises the diversity of contacts, and in dependence on the diversity of contacts there arises the diversity of feelings.”
sn35.130:2.12 | P: Sattamaṁ. | T: (none)


